  "question": {
    "text": "An object of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> is projected with a velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> at an angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, to the horizontal. At the highest point of its path, the projectile explodes and breaks up into two fragments of masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The fragments separate horizontally after the explosion, which releases internal energy such that the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mi>E</mi></math> of the system af the highest point is doubled. The separation between the two fragments when they reach the ground is"
  },
